WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.840
This is the DP Pod, episode 40. I'm Dan Pere, he's Patrick Novak. I spent this morning staring at a tomato plant on the balcony, trying to decide if it wanted more sun or less. That's the most chemist thing you've said all week, just water it. Fair. Egyptian farmers just answered a version of that question at farm scale, though. Same field, food and power. Panels over the crop rows, shade that cuts water use, electricity off the same acre.

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:52.180
That's the show. All right, time for the positive papers, start with the farms. Local cooperatives in Egypt, with research partners, are mounting solar panels above vegetable rows, so one plot does two jobs. The shade drops soil evaporation, which in an erud zone can be less irrigation, and they're doing it on marginal land without clearing anything new.

00:00:52.180 --> 00:01:00.140
Dual use, that's a combo flight, passengers and freight on the same airframe, works if the weight and balance is right, fails if you guess.

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:07.960
That's actually the honest version. Panel height and crop choice have to be tuned to the site, or the yields go the wrong way.

00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:10.060
Wait, they're getting better yields?

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:13.060
Stable or improved, for certain vegetables?

00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:15.220
Improved, undershade?

00:01:15.220 --> 00:01:19.420
Early field results, not every crop likes the partial shade.

00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:22.460
Okay, that's genuinely wild if it holds.

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:32.120
The mechanism is a microclimate. Elevated panels hold moisture in the soil, while the same inverters and wiring push power to nearby grids.

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:35.240
Steady output, no extra ground broken.

00:01:35.660 --> 00:01:36.840
So the plants drink less?

00:01:37.540 --> 00:01:39.640
That's the operator translation, yes.

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:42.640
I'm going to need that on a shirt.

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:46.440
The builders I want named are those cooperative teams.

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:51.880
They're matching varieties to real fields and local markets and water tables, not a greenhouse.

00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:54.500
That's the part that survives contact.

00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:56.540
Here's how this fails in the field.

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:01.840
If the grid hookup doesn't keep pace, you've built expensive shade with nowhere to send the electrons.

00:02:02.540 --> 00:02:08.820
And long-term soil health data is still being collected, which is the kind of thing I'd want before I bet a whole valley on it.

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:15.840
Both true, these are demonstration sites, not a national rollout, and results vary by region and season.

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:19.600
Yesterday, we were already on the Global Renewables build-out.

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:23.740
Today's pilots add a land use layer on top of that cost curve.

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:27.180
So how big is this, really? A footnote or a new chapter?

00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:31.860
I think it's a chapter. Land is the constraint nobody wants to talk about.

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:35.760
Food versus power is a fake fight if the same acre can do both.

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:38.600
I like the mechanism. I don't buy the scale yet.

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:43.720
Pilots that still need height tuning and crop matching are a long way from changing a country's water budget.

00:02:44.180 --> 00:02:47.100
I'll give you the nationwide point. They are not there.

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:51.860
What I will not give you is treating land use efficiency as a side quest.

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:57.780
Watch whether utilities in other arid regions copy the layout once the Egyptian numbers get published.

00:02:58.220 --> 00:03:00.980
Deal. We watch the copycats not the press release.

00:03:00.980 --> 00:03:07.120
Second paper is physics and the number is 420 kilometers.

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:08.840
Wait, say that again.

00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:11.560
420 of what?

00:03:11.860 --> 00:03:16.600
Optical fiber entangled quantum memories, a new distance record.

00:03:16.900 --> 00:03:18.320
That city to city range.

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:23.120
That's why it made the show. Teams including the Technical University of Munich

00:03:23.120 --> 00:03:28.540
through the Munich Center for Quantum Science and Technology used existing fiber,

00:03:28.540 --> 00:03:32.940
not a custom lab cable, and the states stayed intact the whole way.

00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:38.060
Existing fiber. There's your combo flight again. Same wire, two jobs.

00:03:38.620 --> 00:03:40.920
I walked into that.

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:42.160
You really did.

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:48.180
The photonic crystal trick is that it guides single photons more precisely inside the glass.

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:52.180
So fewer of them get lost before they hit the next memory node.

00:03:52.180 --> 00:03:55.840
Earlier attempts lost coherence after tens of kilometers.

00:03:56.460 --> 00:03:58.380
This one held across the full span.

00:03:58.820 --> 00:04:03.460
Tens of kilometers to 420. That's not a nudge. That's a different airplane.

00:04:03.980 --> 00:04:08.960
The builders engineered the loss reduction steps and then tested them on real fiber routes.

00:04:09.640 --> 00:04:14.320
I get genuinely giddy about that and I can already hear you asking what still breaks.

00:04:14.840 --> 00:04:19.200
Specialized cooling and alignment gear that is not sitting in a normal telecom room

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:24.220
and they still need quantum repeaters that can store and forward the state without measuring it.

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:26.500
That's early lab work, not a product.

00:04:26.920 --> 00:04:29.740
Correct. This is not a network you can buy a ticket on.

00:04:30.200 --> 00:04:33.300
It's a link that survived a distance that used to kill the state.

00:04:33.820 --> 00:04:38.680
Error correction and those repeaters are still required before you get anything like a real system.

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:44.900
For a regular person, this is some day a data center exchanges a key that cannot be copied without someone noticing.

00:04:45.500 --> 00:04:46.360
Cool. Distant.

00:04:46.360 --> 00:04:50.300
I think you're under selling the reuse. They didn't pour a new spine.

00:04:50.740 --> 00:04:56.840
They showed the state can ride glass that's already in the ground, which is how city to city even becomes thinkable.

00:04:57.360 --> 00:05:02.480
I'll take the reuse seriously. I will not pretend city scale quantum mail is next quarter.

00:05:03.080 --> 00:05:08.820
Fair. The open question is which group turns the lab link into a pilot that handles actual traffic.

00:05:09.320 --> 00:05:11.140
That's the next number worth watching.

00:05:11.140 --> 00:05:16.640
Third paper is money and this is the one that decides whether any of the above has somewhere to plug in.

00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:24.220
Global renewable investment is projected to hit a new high this year, even after announced federal spending cuts in the United States.

00:05:24.780 --> 00:05:29.820
Private capital into solar, wind and storage on the back of falling hardware costs.

00:05:30.260 --> 00:05:30.860
Projected.

00:05:31.340 --> 00:05:35.200
Projected. Developers and utilities are the ones signing the contracts.

00:05:35.720 --> 00:05:37.900
On cheaper modules, not new subsidies.

00:05:37.900 --> 00:05:44.920
That's the claim. Module and battery prices dropped enough that the return still work without a fresh federal check.

00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:50.400
Okay. That's the part that gets me. Those power purchase agreements are the thing that actually ships.

00:05:50.960 --> 00:05:53.260
A forecast without a signature is a slide deck.

00:05:53.740 --> 00:06:01.040
The mechanism is almost boring, which I love. When the hardware is cheap enough, you don't need the subsidy to make the spreadsheet close.

00:06:01.640 --> 00:06:04.780
Private money steps in where federal dollars step back.

00:06:04.780 --> 00:06:08.100
Commodity prices and permitting can still knock at sideways.

00:06:08.580 --> 00:06:12.260
And a supply chains clog again, that record number gets revised.

00:06:12.660 --> 00:06:16.560
I've watched record year forecast miss because one permit sat on a desk.

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:18.280
Honest caveat, yes.

00:06:18.720 --> 00:06:24.360
The open question is whether state policy and corporate buying hold the pace if federal support stays reduced.

00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:31.960
Regions that leaned on tax credits now under discussion may see those green tariff choices show up slower in the rate filings.

00:06:31.960 --> 00:06:38.700
See, this is the bigger story, not because it's sexier, because signed contracts are how capacity appears on a grid

00:06:38.700 --> 00:06:42.600
and lower wholesale prices are what a regular person actually feels.

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:48.360
I'll concede the shipping point. Those agreements are what turn a forecast into steel in the ground.

00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:52.580
What I won't concede is that the Egyptian pilots are a side show.

00:06:53.300 --> 00:06:58.240
If investment keeps climbing and landfights get worse, dual use is how you keep building.

00:06:58.240 --> 00:07:03.120
Steel men, you're saying the money needs a place to land that doesn't eat farmland.

00:07:03.680 --> 00:07:07.700
Exactly, same instinct as that fiber, use what's already there.

00:07:08.140 --> 00:07:10.780
Then we agree on the pairing and disagree on the weight.

00:07:11.400 --> 00:07:13.580
I'll watch the contracts, you watch the copycats.

00:07:13.980 --> 00:07:16.600
That's a better landing than either of us started with.

